        {
            "source_entity_instance": "summer droughts",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Munich",
            "target_entity_type": "City",
            "paragraph": "Munich has already experienced a warming of approximately 1.0 \u00b0C in its annual mean temperature when comparing the periods 1961\u20131990 and 1991\u20132020. Climate projections based on the RCPs from the IPCC Fifth Assessment Report (2014) indicate a continued rise in temperature throughout the 21 st century. Under the RCP 8.5 scenario, the annual mean temperature in Munich is projected to increase by an additional 1.6 \u00b0C in the near future (2041\u20132050) relative to the 1991-2020 baseline. By the late 21 st century (2081-\u20132090), a substantial temperature increase of approximately 3.2 \u00b0C is expected, resulting in an annual mean temperature of around 15.2 \u00b0C. Such warming, combined with prolonged summer droughts, may exacerbate heat stress in urban areas and challenge the capacity of trees to provide cooling and other ecosystem services. To address these challenges, this study builds on the interdisciplinary project \"100 Places: M\" (Far\u00edas and Keller, 2020), which systematically documented over 100 urban squares across Munich, integrating urban design, ecological, and socio-anthropological perspectives to assess their potential for climate adaptation.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
